            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"The glory, presence, and righteous revelation of God, most specifically fulfilled in Jesus Christ as the Sun of Righteousness who brings salvation and illumination.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Malachi 4:2\",\"Psalm 84:11\",\"Revelation 1:16\",\"Revelation 21:23\",\"Matthew 17:2\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Supreme governing authorities, rulers, or empires, often whose fall or judgment is depicted by the sun being darkened.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 37:9\",\"Isaiah 13:10\",\"Matthew 24:29\",\"Revelation 6:12\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Persecution, trials, or the harshness of temporal life that tests faith.\",\"basis\":[\"Matthew 13:6\",\"James 1:11\",\"Psalm 121:6\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"The natural order, timekeeping, and God's general revelation or common grace to all creation.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 1:14-16\",\"Psalm 19:1-6\",\"Matthew 5:45\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"While the messianic association with Christ as the Sun of Righteousness is the dominant theological and devotional symbol, apocalyptic literature frequently uses the sun to represent cosmic order or governing authorities which are disrupted during divine judgment. Parabolic literature occasionally uses the scorching sun as a negative symbol for persecution.\"}",
